From eda22bac201bd4be3e872cdb12b074b311d82763 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 17:04:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] MV-HEVC: both views of a 3D video in
 HEVC decoded and put side by side

A 3D video in MV-HEVC (spatial video of an iPhone or a Vision Pro, the 3D
films made for the Vision Pro) codes its second eye as a second layer of
the HEVC stream. FFmpeg's own HEVC decoder gives both views from 7.1 on,
when its option view_ids asks for them (two layers at most), each view a
picture with AV_FRAME_DATA_VIEW_ID and, where the stream has a 3D
reference displays information SEI, AVStereo3D with its eye. Hades played
the base view only, in 2D.

It now goes the way of 3D Blu-ray MVC (KODI::MULTIVIEW):

- The track: the video parameter set in the private data declares two
  layers whose only scalability is multiview, as FFmpeg decodes them
  (MvHevcSecondLayer(), read as ITU-T H.265 7.3.2.1 and F.7.3.2.1.1 lay
  it out), and the sequence and picture parameter sets of the second
  layer are there too: in MP4/MOV from 'lhvC', which FFmpeg appends to
  the record. A Matroska track made by FFmpeg or mkvmerge keeps the base
  layer's sets only (measured with FFmpeg 9.1: the second view is then
  "PPS id out of range"), and stays 2D, with the reason in the log.
  Three views or more, another scalability or an alpha layer: 2D.
- The eye: the container's 'stri' (eyes reversed) or Matroska StereoMode,
  then what each base picture says of its own eye, which is believed
  first (EyeOfBaseView()).
- The decoder: FFmpeg's "hevc" with view_ids "-1", by the processor, never
  with a hardware decoder, which would be given the slices of the second
  view without the pictures they are predicted from.
- Dolby Vision profile 20 with an IPT-PQ-c2 base layer is kept to its
  base view, rebuilt by the bridge (KeptToOneView()); the player
  information says so.
- The setting "Decode both views" and "Switch the display to 3D" apply as
  for MVC.

And for MVC and MV-HEVC both: where the processor does not decode both
views in time, the player falls behind and drops pictures again and
again. After two seconds of grace from a start or a seek, two windows of
four seconds in a row in which one picture in eight or more went out
while the player asked for drops (DVD_CODEC_CTRL_DROP_ANY, never at
another speed or before a seek target) give the second view up: the
stream goes back to its base view in 2D, with hardware decoding where
there is (Paced()). The log says so, and the player information
("second_view", strings 54361 and 54362 of Estuary, English and Spanish).

The setting texts name MV-HEVC. Tested with the ITU-T MV-HEVC conformance
streams on FFmpeg 9.1 (tools/regress/mvhevc).
